---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'docsindex' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Documentation"
  description="Three paths: trying it out, setting it up for a repository, and seeing where platform-level enforcement is headed.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b></p>
          <h1>Documentation</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Three ways in. Start with the one that fits what you're trying to do.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm trying it out</h2>
          <p>You want to see how it works on your own pull requests, without needing anyone else's permission.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('install', 'en')}><span class="pn">Install the extension</span><span class="pd">Get it from your browser's extension store, or use a release archive if building locally isn't an option. You'll be up and running in minutes.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('labels', 'en')}><span class="pn">Labels &amp; decorations</span><span class="pd">Meet the thirteen labels, learn what each one means, and understand when a comment becomes blocking.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm setting it up for a repository</h2>
          <p>Your team is on board. Now you want everyone following the same rules.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('configure', 'en')}><span class="pn">Configure</span><span class="pd">One file on the default branch. Learn which settings matter, which gotchas to watch for, and how the three levels of configuration work together.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('adoption', 'en')}><span class="pn">Adoption</span><span class="pd">assist → warn → enforce. See what changes at each stage and how to roll back quickly if you need to.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>I'm wondering what happens next</h2>
          <p>You want the convention applied consistently, regardless of who has installed what — and you're curious where enforcement is headed.</p>
          <div class="paths">
            <a class="path" href={href('server', 'en')}><span class="pn">Enforce (roadmap)</span><span class="pd">Why platform-level enforcement isn't shipped yet, what already works without it, and how the eventual implementation is being kept open.</span></a>
            <a class="path" href={href('troubleshooting', 'en')}><span class="pn">Troubleshooting</span><span class="pd">Understand the banners and the few common surprises you're likely to run into the first time.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Reference</h2>
          <p>Stable references designed for tools as well as people.</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>The configuration schema</strong> is available at <code>conventionalcomments.io/schema/v1.json</code>. Add it to your file's <code>$schema</code> to get autocompletion and inline validation in your editor.</li>
            <li><strong>The Conventional Comments specification</strong> lives at <a href="https://conventionalcomments.org/">conventionalcomments.org</a>. This site provides independent tooling around it and does not define or represent the specification.</li>
            <li><strong>The source code</strong> is available under Apache-2.0 and includes the normative specification and the full set of acceptance criteria.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
